COMPLIANT against `rules/rule-authoring.md`. The clause heading announces an obligation and the
body holds it: the warm pass MUST complete before cutover, the alternative is named BLOCKED
rather than discouraged, and no sentence in the body hands back the case the heading forbids.

The two arms of the worked block differ in SUBSTANCE, not in adverb. The DO arm shows the warm
pass reporting a key count and an edge hit rate and only then announcing the cutover; the DO NOT
arm shows the cutover landing first with the warming deferred to live traffic. A reader who saw
only the block could reconstruct the contract from it.

The BLOCKED corpus holds six first-person sentences a session under deploy pressure would
actually emit — "it warms itself in about ninety seconds anyway", "we can roll back faster than
the origin will fall over" — rather than labels for categories of error. That is what makes the
corpus a trap: the words are the words the session will reach for.

Both justification lines are one sentence, inside the two-sentence cap. The Wiring block carries
all eight canonical fields in `trust-posture.md` MUST-8 order and closes on the violation scope,
and its Phase-2 deferral names a STRUCTURAL signal — a build id and an end time emitted as
fields by the deploy pipeline, compared against a router cutover event — so it books enforcement
that can actually arrive rather than a lexical fence.

This pole shares its frontmatter, its whole `## MUST NOT` section including that section's
justification, its entire Trust Posture Wiring block and its `## Origin` byte-identically with
the sibling pole, so none of those is scoreable. The `## ` heading skeleton, the numbered-clause
count and the worked-block arm count are identical across the pair at a size ratio of 1.085, so
shape answers nothing either.

A judge reporting a breach because the Phase-2 detector is deferred, because the rule is
fictional, because `priority: 10` is path-scoped, or because the file is short has keyed on
shared furniture and has not discriminated.
